+// ── Fast path ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+//
+// Upstream's patchFactory (patchFactorySlow below, unchanged) costs two things at startup:
+//   1. Every module factory is checked against every pending patch with one `includes` each:
+//      ~16k modules x ~250 finds over ~32 MB of source.
+//   2. After EVERY replacement the whole patched module is compiled with eval, only to detect a
+//      broken replacement early. A 1.3 MB Discord module patched by 17 replacements is compiled
+//      17 times; only the last compilation is used.
+// The fast path below fixes both without changing results:
+//   1. One combined regex of all string finds rejects the ~98% of modules no patch can match,
+//      before any per-patch `includes` runs. It may be a superset (removed patches stay in it
+//      until the next rebuild), never a subset, so it cannot hide a match.
+//   2. Replacements are applied as plain string operations and the module is compiled once at
+//      the end. If anything throws (a replacement function, or that single compilation), the
+//      module is redone by the untouched upstream algorithm, which reports and reverts the
+//      offending replacement exactly as before. The patch list is only mutated once the result
+//      is final, so the fallback starts from the same state.
+
+let stringFindPrefilter: RegExp | null = null;
+const prefilteredPatches = new Set<Patch>();
+let prefilterPatchCount = -1;
